// sin/cos for the Park transform. Functions resolve against this file's own
// imports, not the consumer's, so the import has to be here.
from <math> import sin, cos;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  PMSM — permanent-magnet synchronous machine, dq model with a shaft port
//
//  The general salient-pole machine. Ld != Lq (normally Lq > Ld) is an
//  interior-magnet machine (IPMSM), with reluctance torque; Ld == Lq is a
//  surface-magnet machine (SPMSM), with magnet torque only. The two wrappers
//  IPMSM and SPMSM in this directory are the same model under those names.
//
//      va ──┐                              ┌─────────────────────┐
//      vb ──┤ star, isolated neutral       │ C<J>   B·ω    T_em  │── shaft
//      vc ──┘ (no zero-sequence path)      └─────────────────────┘
//
//  Electrical, in the rotor (dq) frame, d axis on the magnet:
//
//      vd = Rs·id + Ld·did/dt − ωe·Lq·iq
//      vq = Rs·iq + Lq·diq/dt + ωe·(Ld·id + flux_m)
//      ωe = p·ωm,   θe = θ0 + ∫ωe dt
//
//  Each axis is a voltage source in parallel with its inductor, driven with
//  vd − Rs·id − ed (and likewise for q), the axis current being the
//  inductor's own branch current — so Rs = 0 is allowed.
//
//  Transforms are amplitude-invariant (Clarke 2/3 scaling): a balanced set of
//  phase currents of peak Î gives |id + j·iq| = Î, and the same for voltages.
//  Power is therefore P = 1.5·(vd·id + vq·iq), which is where the 1.5 in the
//  torque comes from:
//
//      T_em = 1.5·p·(flux_m·iq + (Ld − Lq)·id·iq)
//
//  Terminals. The windings are star-connected with an isolated neutral and no
//  neutral pin: the three phase currents always sum to zero, and the neutral
//  voltage is taken as the mean of va, vb, vc. Each terminal is a current
//  source to ground, so
//    - no phase may be left completely open (a node with only a current
//      source on it is singular): a phase that is to carry no current still
//      needs a path, e.g. a large resistor;
//    - the machine gives the terminals no reference of its own: whatever
//      drives them must tie them to ground somewhere (an inverter's DC link,
//      grounded sources, a grounded star point of a load).
//
//  Shaft. The mechanics use the circuit analogy
//
//      V(shaft) = ωm, mechanical speed in rad/s     (1 V = 1 rad/s)
//      current  = torque in N·m                     (1 A = 1 N·m)
//      C = inertia (kg·m²),  conductance = viscous friction (N·m·s/rad)
//
//  so the rotor's own J and B sit inside the model, and the electromagnetic
//  torque is a current source into the shaft node. On the shaft node:
//
//      J·dωm/dt = T_em − B·ωm − T_shaft
//
//  where T_shaft is the current that leaves the model through the shaft pin
//  into whatever is attached — the torque the machine delivers to its load.
//  Motoring torque is positive (it accelerates ωm in the positive direction).
//  A current that enters the model through the shaft pin is a torque driving
//  the rotor (a prime mover). External loads are ordinary elements from shaft
//  to ground:
//
//      C<J_load>()                [shaft, gnd];  // load inertia
//      R<1/B_load>()              [shaft, gnd];  // viscous friction
//      current_source<T_L>()      [shaft, gnd];  // constant load torque T_L
//      current_source<>(t_l)      [shaft, gnd];  // driven load torque
//      voltage_source vw<>(w_cmd) [shaft, gnd];  // impose the speed
//
//  and two machines on one shaft node share it, inertias and torques adding.
//  The torque a speed source absorbs is I(vw) — positive when the machine
//  drives it, negative when it drives the machine as a generator. (Start an
//  imposed speed from 0 and ramp it: a fixed nonzero source across the rotor
//  inertia at t = 0 has to charge it in one step, which the bias-point solve
//  reports as not converged, although the transient then runs correctly.
//  The operating-point solve also cannot yet reach a node above about 250 V,
//  i.e. a shaft above about 250 rad/s at t = 0 with .op.)
//
//  Example — a machine driving extra inertia, viscous friction and a
//  constant 0.2 N·m load:
//
//      wire a, b, c, shaft;
//      state double w = 0, id = 0, iq = 0, th = 0, te = 0;
//      module m = PMSM<0.5, 2m, 3m, 0.05, 4, 1e-4, 1e-5, 0>
//                     (w, id, iq, th, te)[a, b, c, shaft];
//      C<5e-4>()             [shaft, gnd];   // load inertia, kg·m²
//      R<2000>()             [shaft, gnd];   // load friction 5e-4 N·m·s/rad
//      current_source<0.2>() [shaft, gnd];   // load torque, N·m
//
//  B = 0 is always fine (friction is a VCCS, not R<1/B>). J = 0 is fine when
//  something else sets the speed — B > 0 (the speed then follows the torque
//  algebraically), an attached inertia, or a speed source. With J = 0, B = 0
//  and nothing on the shaft the rotor is massless and free: the shaft
//  equation degenerates to T_em = 0 and the rotor snaps to whatever angle
//  gives zero torque. That solves, but it is rarely the machine you meant.
//
//  Rotor angle. θe is the electrical angle of the d axis (the magnet's north
//  pole) ahead of the phase-a axis; θ0 is its value at t = 0, so θ0 = 0 puts
//  the magnet on phase a. θe = θ0 + idt(ωe): an integrator state of the
//  solver, not a circuit node, so the operating point (.op) has θe = θ0
//  exactly at any speed, and no node ever carries a large angle. It is never
//  wrapped and grows without bound with the rotation. That is harmless: it
//  only enters through sin and cos, and a double still resolves 2e-9 rad at
//  θe = 1e7 rad (hours of rotation at thousands of rad/s).
//
//  Solver. Run it under a Newton solver — .solver(bdf2), ndf2 or
//  trapezoidal. The default, euler_fixed, evaluates the model once per step
//  from the previous solution, so the angle lags by ωe·dt and every dq
//  quantity carries a first-order error (2 % of id at 400 rad/s electrical
//  and dt = 10 µs, in testing); the Newton solvers match the analytic steady
//  state to 1e-6.
//
//  Parameters
//      Rs      stator resistance per phase, Ohm (0 allowed)
//      Ld, Lq  d- and q-axis inductance, H (both > 0)
//      flux_m  permanent-magnet flux linkage, Wb: the peak flux linked by one
//              phase. Equals Ke/p with Ke the peak phase back-EMF constant in
//              V·s/rad (mechanical); from a line-to-line RMS constant Ke_LL
//              (V per rad/s), flux_m = Ke_LL·√2/(√3·p).
//      p       number of pole PAIRS (an 8-pole machine has p = 4)
//      J       rotor inertia, kg·m² (0 allowed — see above)
//      B       rotor viscous friction, N·m·s/rad (0 allowed)
//      theta0  initial electrical rotor angle θe(0), rad
//
//  Outputs (logic)
//      omega_m_out  mechanical speed ωm, rad/s (= V(shaft))
//      i_d_out      d-axis current, A
//      i_q_out      q-axis current, A
//      theta_e_out  electrical angle θe, rad (unwrapped)
//      torque_out   electromagnetic torque T_em, N·m (before rotor friction)
//
//  Not modelled: magnetic saturation and the cross-coupling of Ld and Lq
//  with current (both are constants), iron loss, cogging torque and
//  harmonics in the back-EMF (the EMF is sinusoidal), temperature (Rs and
//  flux_m are fixed), damper windings, and any zero-sequence path.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

analog module PMSM<double Rs, double Ld, double Lq, double flux_m, double p,
                   double J, double B, double theta0>
    (output double omega_m_out, output double i_d_out, output double i_q_out,
     output double theta_e_out, output double torque_out)
    [wire va, wire vb, wire vc, wire shaft]
{
    wire d1, q1;

    // ── Mechanics, on the shaft node ────────────────────────────────────────
    // Inertia J·dωm/dt is a capacitor; friction B·ωm a conductance B, written
    // as a VCCS sensing its own terminals so that B = 0 is simply no current
    // (R<1/B> would divide by zero).
    C<J>()                   [shaft, gnd];
    VCCS<B>()                [shaft, gnd, shaft, gnd];

    double omega_m = V(shaft);
    double omega_e = p * omega_m;

    // ── Electrical angle: θe = θ0 + ∫ p·ωm dt ───────────────────────────────
    double theta_e = theta0 + idt(omega_e);
    double c_th = cos(theta_e);
    double s_th = sin(theta_e);

    // ── Terminal voltages → dq (Clarke, amplitude-invariant, then Park) ─────
    double v_n = (V(va) + V(vb) + V(vc)) / 3.0;   // isolated star point
    double van = V(va) - v_n;
    double vbn = V(vb) - v_n;

    double v_alpha = van;
    double v_beta  = (van + 2.0 * vbn) / (3.0**0.5);

    double v_d =  v_alpha * c_th + v_beta * s_th;
    double v_q = -v_alpha * s_th + v_beta * c_th;

    // ── dq windings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // The axis currents are the inductors' branch currents.
    double i_d = I(Ld_coil);
    double i_q = I(Lq_coil);

    double e_d = -omega_e * Lq * i_q;                  // speed voltage, d
    double e_q =  omega_e * (Ld * i_d + flux_m);       // speed voltage, q

    // Ld·did/dt = vd − Rs·id − ed, and likewise for q.
    double v_Ld = v_d - Rs * i_d - e_d;
    double v_Lq = v_q - Rs * i_q - e_q;
    voltage_source<>(v_Ld) [d1, gnd];
    L Ld_coil<Ld>()        [d1, gnd];
    voltage_source<>(v_Lq) [q1, gnd];
    L Lq_coil<Lq>()        [q1, gnd];

    // ── dq currents → terminals (inverse Park, inverse Clarke) ─────────────
    double i_alpha = i_d * c_th - i_q * s_th;
    double i_beta  = i_d * s_th + i_q * c_th;

    double i_a = i_alpha;
    double i_b = -0.5 * i_alpha + ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * i_beta;
    double i_c = -0.5 * i_alpha - ((3.0**0.5) / 2.0) * i_beta;

    // Motor convention: positive current flows into the terminal.
    current_source<>(i_a) [va, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_b) [vb, gnd];
    current_source<>(i_c) [vc, gnd];

    // ── Electromagnetic torque, into the shaft ──────────────────────────────
    double torque = 1.5 * p * (flux_m * i_q + (Ld - Lq) * i_d * i_q);
    current_source<>(torque) [gnd, shaft];

    omega_m_out = omega_m;
    i_d_out     = i_d;
    i_q_out     = i_q;
    theta_e_out = theta_e;
    torque_out  = torque;
}
